window.docContents[136].resolve({"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Fs.Fcntl/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Fs___Fcntl":{"contents":"Paper › arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Fs › Fcntl.leanTier 3 · arm64-semantics\n\nTier 3 · arm64-semantics used by the symbolic proof 2 headline theorems depend on it 27 of 55 lines trusted source ↗Trusted, reviewed with the engine. The AArch64 model in arm64-semantics is Tier 3: it is reviewed in its own repository (check-trust-guards). The declarations that the headline statements use are shown for completeness.\n\nLinux fcntl SyscallModels the Linux AArch64 fcntl syscall (__NR_fcntl = 25, fs/fcntl.c:590, include/uapi/asm-generic/fcntl.h:111) for F_GETFD (1) and F_SETFD (2, FD_CLOEXEC = 1).\n\n\n\ndef SYS_FCNTLL25–26 ↗#Linux AArch64 fcntl syscall number (__NR_fcntl = 25, include/uapi/asm-generic/unistd.h:99).\n\ndef F_GETFD_W32L28–29 ↗#Linux fcntl.h commands F_GETFD (1) and F_SETFD (2).\n\ndef F_SETFD_W32L30 ↗#\n\ndef execFcntlL32–53 ↗#fcntl(fd, cmd, arg): models F_GETFD (1) and F_SETFD (2, updating FdEntry.cloexec := (arg &&& 1) != 0) on open .localFile or .localDir descriptors, and faults on all other descriptor kinds or commands.\n\n\n\n","context":"AlphaProof Code: Verifying Signal’s Security at the Machine-Code Level","header":"arm64-semantics/Arm64/Trusted/Syscalls/Fs/Fcntl.lean","id":"/Tier-3___-trusted___-not-read/AArch64-semantics/Arm64.Trusted.Syscalls.Fs.Fcntl/#file-Arm64___Trusted___Syscalls___Fs___Fcntl"}});